<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG BUILD TYPE ONLY - see app/src/debug/AndroidManifest.xml, which is where this gets wired
  in via android:networkSecurityConfig on <application>. Manifest merger only applies a debug
  source set's manifest to debug builds, so a release build never sees this attribute at all and
  keeps Android API 28+'s default: plaintext HTTP blocked everywhere, no opt-in file present.

  WHY THIS EXISTS: the Django engine (backend/engine/EngineAuth.kt, EngineConfig) runs on a
  laptop on the LAN during development - `http://192.168.1.117:8000` observed on the A25
  2026-09-02 - and has no TLS certificate to serve. Without this file every sign-in attempt from
  a debug build throws `CLEARTEXT communication to 192.168.1.117 not permitted by network
  security policy` before a single byte reaches the laptop. Production (Cloud Run) is HTTPS and
  is untouched by this file either way.

  SCOPE, AND WHY IT ISN'T NARROWER: a <domain-config> only matches literal domain names or
  wildcarded subdomains (<domain>, optionally with wildcardIncludeSubdomains) - it has no syntax
  for an IP-address range or a CIDR block, so "any 192.168.x.x / 10.x.x.x address" cannot be
  expressed as a domain-config allowlist. Enumerating every octet LEGION's LAN might hand out
  (192.168.0.0-255, 192.168.1.0-255, 10.0.0.0-255, ... 172.16-31.x.x, plus 127.0.0.1 and the
  10.0.2.2 emulator-host alias) would need thousands of <domain> entries, would still miss
  whatever subnet the laptop is on next time DHCP hands out a different one, and would silently
  stop working the day the laptop's address changes - exactly the kind of stale-and-wrong
  guardrail CLAUDE.md warns against. A base-config is therefore used instead: cleartext permitted
  to ANY host, for THIS BUILD TYPE ONLY. This is acceptable specifically because debug is never
  distributed - it is not the build a stranger sideloads under clone-and-run, and it is not what
  a Play or other store listing would ship (there is no listing; CLAUDE.md section 2). The
  narrower alternative, if one is ever found that survives a moving LAN address without becoming
  a second thing to keep in sync, should replace this comment and this file together.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
